                    Python logging levels
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-7 max-w-[54ch] font-display text-[17px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted md:text-lg">
                    What each level means, why your debug logs don't print, and how to set the level from code, a flag, an environment variable or a config.
                </p>
                <div class="mt-7 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[10px] text-fg-muted">
                    <a href={authorUrl} rel="noopener" class="flex items-center gap-2 transition-colors hover:text-fg">
                        <img src={author.avatar} alt="" width="20" height="20" class="h-5 w-5 rounded-full ring-1 ring-border" />
                        <span>By {author.name}</span>
                    </a>
                    <time datetime="2026-10-20">Updated {updatedLabel}</time>
                </div>
            </div>
        </header>

        <nav aria-label="Guide sections" class="border-b border-border bg-bg-deep">
            <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-6 overflow-x-auto px-6 py-4 font-mono text-[10px] whitespace-nowrap text-fg-muted">
                {sectionNav.map((h) => (
                    <a href={`#${h.slug}`} class="transition-colors hover:text-primary">{h.text}</a>
                ))}
            </div>
        </nav>

        <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-6xl gap-12 px-6 py-16 md:py-24 lg:grid-cols-[14rem_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-16">
            <aside class="hidden lg:block">
                <TableOfContents headings={toc} class="sticky top-28" />
            </aside>

            <article class="pylog-guide min-w-0">
                <section id="levels" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>01</span> · Definition</p>
                    <h2>The Python logging levels</h2>

                    <div class="guide-answer">
                        <p>A <strong>logging level</strong> is a number that says how serious a log message is. Python drops every message below the level you set, and the default is WARNING, so <code>debug()</code> and <code>info()</code> print nothing until you lower it.</p>
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        The <code>logging</code> module defines five levels as integer constants: <code>logging.DEBUG</code> is 10, <code>logging.CRITICAL</code> is 50. Comparisons are numeric, so a logger set to <code>logging.INFO</code> handles every record with a level of 20 or more.
                    </p>

                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Level</th><th>Value</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Shown by default</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {levels.map((l) => (
                                    <tr class:list={[{ "is-hidden": !l.shown }]}>
                                        <td><span class="lvl-badge" style={`--lvl: ${l.color}`}>{l.name}</span></td>
                                        <td class="mono">{l.value}</td>
                                        <td>{l.use}</td>
                                        <td class="mono">{l.shown ? "Yes" : "No"}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        <code>WARN</code> and <code>FATAL</code> are accepted aliases for <code>WARNING</code> and <code>CRITICAL</code>. <code>logger.exception()</code> logs at ERROR and appends the current traceback, so call it inside an <code>except</code> block. A sixth constant, <code>NOTSET</code> (0), is not a severity: on a logger it means "use my parent's level", on a handler it means "accept every record".
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">Choosing a level</h3>
                    <p>
                        Ask whether you want the line in production every day. If yes, it is INFO. If only while chasing a bug, DEBUG. WARNING and above should be rare enough that someone reads each one: a retry that succeeded is a WARNING, a request that failed is an ERROR, and a process that cannot continue is CRITICAL.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="default" class="scroll-mt-24">The default level is WARNING</h3>
                    <p>
                        Every logger sits in a tree under the <strong>root logger</strong>, which Python creates at WARNING. A logger from <code>logging.getLogger("app")</code> starts with no level of its own and uses the root's, which is why INFO and DEBUG messages print nothing in a fresh script. <code>log.getEffectiveLevel()</code> returns the level a logger actually uses.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="logger-vs-handler" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>02</span> · How levels filter</p>
                    <h2>Logger level vs handler level</h2>
                    <p>
                        A record passes two levels. The <strong>logger level</strong> decides whether the record is created at all. Each <strong>handler level</strong> then decides whether that handler writes it. A handler is the object that sends records somewhere: <code>StreamHandler</code> writes to the terminal, <code>FileHandler</code> to a file.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This script gives the root logger two handlers with different levels, sets the <code>app</code> logger to ERROR and turns on DEBUG for <code>app.db</code> only:
                    </p>
                    <div class="code-file">
                        <p class="code-file__name">levels.py</p>
                        <pre><code set:html={code(gatesCode)} /></pre>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(gatesRun, "console")} /></pre>

                    <figure class="gates" aria-label="How the three log calls in levels.py are filtered. db.debug('pool size 5'): the app.db logger is set to DEBUG, so the record passes. It then propagates to app and the root without their levels being checked, and reaches the root's two handlers: the console handler at WARNING skips it and the app.log file handler at DEBUG writes it. api.debug('GET /orders'): app.api has no level, inherits ERROR from app, and drops the record before any handler sees it.">
                        <svg viewBox="0 0 760 292" aria-hidden="true">
                            <defs>
                                <marker id="gates-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                                    <path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z" class="gates__head" />
                                </marker>
                            </defs>

                            {[["0", "Log call"], ["200", "1 Logger level"], ["400", "2 Propagation"], ["600", "3 Handler levels"]].map(([x, label]) => (
                                <text x={x} y="12" class="gates__step">{label}</text>
                            ))}

                            <rect x="0" y="37" width="170" height="50" rx="8" class="gates__box" />
                            <text x="14" y="58" class="gates__mono gates__mono--fg">db.debug(</text>
                            <text x="14" y="75" class="gates__mono">"pool size 5")</text>
                            <line x1="170" y1="62" x2="196" y2="62" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />

                            <rect x="200" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="8" class="gates__box gates__box--pass" />
                            <text x="214" y="56" class="gates__title">app.db</text>
                            <text x="214" y="76" class="gates__mono">level <tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.DEBUG}`}>DEBUG</tspan>: passes</text>
                            <line x1="360" y1="62" x2="396" y2="62" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />

                            <rect x="400" y="36" width="160" height="52" rx="8" class="gates__box" />
                            <text x="414" y="58" class="gates__title">app.db</text>
                            <text x="414" y="76" class="gates__mono">no handlers</text>
                            <line x1="480" y1="88" x2="480" y2="106" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />
                            <rect x="400" y="108" width="160" height="52" rx="8" class="gates__box gates__box--skip" />
                            <text x="414" y="130" class="gates__title">app</text>
                            <text x="414" y="148" class="gates__mono"><tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.ERROR}`}>ERROR</tspan>, not checked</text>
                            <line x1="480" y1="160" x2="480" y2="178" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />
                            <rect x="400" y="180" width="160" height="52" rx="8" class="gates__box gates__box--skip" />
                            <text x="414" y="202" class="gates__title">root</text>
                            <text x="414" y="220" class="gates__mono"><tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.WARNING}`}>WARNING</tspan>, not checked</text>

                            <line x1="560" y1="200" x2="596" y2="170" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />
                            <line x1="560" y1="212" x2="596" y2="240" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />
                            <g class="gates__dropped">
                                <rect x="600" y="140" width="160" height="58" rx="8" class="gates__box" />
                                <text x="614" y="160" class="gates__title">console</text>
                                <text x="614" y="176" class="gates__mono">level <tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.WARNING}`}>WARNING</tspan></text>
                                <text x="614" y="191" class="gates__mono">skipped: 10 &lt; 30</text>
                            </g>
                            <rect x="600" y="212" width="160" height="58" rx="8" class="gates__box gates__box--pass" />
                            <text x="614" y="232" class="gates__title">app.log</text>
                            <text x="614" y="248" class="gates__mono">level <tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.DEBUG}`}>DEBUG</tspan></text>
                            <text x="614" y="263" class="gates__mono gates__mono--accent">written</text>

                            <rect x="0" y="230" width="170" height="50" rx="8" class="gates__box" />
                            <text x="14" y="251" class="gates__mono gates__mono--fg">api.debug(</text>
                            <text x="14" y="268" class="gates__mono">"GET /orders")</text>
                            <line x1="170" y1="255" x2="196" y2="255" marker-end="url(#gates-arrow)" class="gates__line" />
                            <g class="gates__dropped">
                                <rect x="200" y="227" width="160" height="56" rx="8" class="gates__box" />
                                <text x="214" y="249" class="gates__title">app.api</text>
                                <text x="214" y="269" class="gates__mono">inherits <tspan class="gates__lvl" style={`--lvl: ${levelColor.ERROR}`}>ERROR</tspan></text>
                            </g>
                            <line x1="360" y1="255" x2="380" y2="255" class="gates__line gates__line--dropped" />
                            <text x="386" y="259" class="gates__mono">dropped: 10 &lt; 40</text>
                        </svg>
                    </figure>
                    <p class="guide-caption">The logger level is checked once, on the logger you called. Propagation hands the record to each ancestor's handlers without checking the ancestors' levels, and each handler applies its own.</p>

                    <h3 id="effective-level" class="scroll-mt-24">Effective level and NOTSET</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>app.api</code> has no level of its own (NOTSET), so it walks up its dotted name until it finds one. Its parent <code>app</code> is set to ERROR, and <code>api.debug("GET /orders")</code> is dropped before any handler sees it. If <code>app</code> had no level either, the search would continue to the root's WARNING. A level on <code>app</code> covers every logger under it unless that logger sets its own, as <code>app.db</code> does.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="propagation" class="scroll-mt-24">Propagation skips parent levels</h3>
                    <p>
                        Once <code>app.db</code> accepts <code>pool size 5</code>, the record goes to the handlers of <code>app.db</code>, then of <code>app</code>, then of the root. The Python docs state that the ancestors' own levels are not checked at that point, only their handlers' levels. So the debug record reaches the root's handlers although <code>app</code> is at ERROR and the root at WARNING, and the file handler writes it. To keep a module's records away from the root's handlers, set <code>logger.propagate = False</code> and give that logger its own handler.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="debug-not-printing" class="scroll-mt-24">Why your debug log doesn't print</h3>
                    <p>
                        When <code>log.debug()</code> prints nothing, one of the two levels is too high, or no handler exists at all. Without a handler on the logger or any ancestor, Python falls back to <code>logging.lastResort</code>, a stderr handler with its level fixed at WARNING:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(noHandlerCode)} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        The debug record passes the logger, reaches <code>lastResort</code>, and is skipped there. Adding a handler fixes it, and <code>basicConfig</code> adds one.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="set-level" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>03</span> · Set the level</p>
                    <h2>Set the logging level in Python</h2>

                    <h3 id="basicconfig" class="scroll-mt-24">basicConfig and setLevel</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>logging.basicConfig(level=...)</code> adds a stderr handler to the root logger and sets the root's level. Call it once, in the program's entry point:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(basicCode)} /></pre>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(basicRun, "console")} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        <code>level</code> takes the constant or its upper-case name: <code>level="DEBUG"</code> works, <code>level="debug"</code> raises <code>ValueError</code>. For one logger, call <code>setLevel</code> on it, for example <code>logging.getLogger("app.db").setLevel(logging.DEBUG)</code>.
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-callout">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">basicConfig runs once</p>
                        <p>
                            If the root logger already has a handler, <code>basicConfig</code> does nothing, level included, and raises no error. Frameworks, test runners and OpenTelemetry setup code often add one first. Call <code>logging.getLogger().setLevel(...)</code> instead, or pass <code>force=True</code>, which removes and closes the existing root handlers before adding its own.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <h3 id="flag-env-config" class="scroll-mt-24">From a flag, a variable or dictConfig</h3>
                    <p>
                        Python reads no log level flag or environment variable on its own, so the program reads one and passes it to the logging setup. A command-line tool takes a flag, a service in a container reads an environment variable, and an application with several handlers and per-module levels describes them in a <code>dictConfig</code> dictionary:
                    </p>
                    <LanguageTabs tabs={setTabs.map(({ id, label }) => ({ id, label }))} label="Where the level comes from" group="maple-guide-log-level-source">
                        {setTabs.map((t) => (
                            <LanguageTab id={t.id}>
                                <p class="code-tab__file">{t.file}</p>
                                <pre><code set:html={code(t.src)} /></pre>
                                <pre class="code-tab__run"><code set:html={code(t.run, "console")} /></pre>
                                <p class="code-tab__note" set:html={t.note} />
                            </LanguageTab>
                        ))}
                    </LanguageTabs>
                    <p>
                        Command-line tools often use a repeatable <code>-v</code> instead of a level name. Each <code>-v</code> lowers the level by one step of 10:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(verboseCode)} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="per-module" class="scroll-mt-24">Per-module levels for noisy libraries</h3>
                    <p>
                        Libraries log under their package name, so one <code>setLevel</code> per library hides its DEBUG and INFO lines while your own code stays verbose. A level on <code>urllib3</code> also covers <code>urllib3.connectionpool</code>. To find the name a library logs under, add <code>%(name)s</code> to the format string and read it off the noisy lines.
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(noisyCode)} /></pre>

                    <h3 id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">Change the level at runtime</h3>
                    <p>
                        <code>setLevel</code> applies from the next log call, so a running service can switch to DEBUG without a restart. This signal handler flips the root level each time the process receives <code>SIGUSR1</code>, which exists on Linux and macOS but not on Windows:
                    </p>
                    <pre><code set:html={code(runtimeCode)} /></pre>
                    <p>
                        Run <code>kill -USR1 &lt;pid&gt;</code> and the service logs <code>log level now DEBUG</code>. Send it again to go back to INFO.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <section id="opentelemetry" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>04</span> · OpenTelemetry</p>
                    <h2>Python log levels in OpenTelemetry and Maple</h2>
                    <p>
                        The OpenTelemetry Python SDK exports <code>logging</code> records through a handler, <code>LoggingHandler</code>. The <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python">Python instrumentation guide</a> attaches one to the root logger in a <code>telemetry.py</code> module, and from then on it is one more handler behind the same two levels. With the root at its default WARNING, INFO records never reach the exporter.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="handler-level" class="scroll-mt-24">Give the exporter its own level</h3>
                    <p>
                        To keep DEBUG in the terminal and send only INFO and above to Maple, set the root low and the exporting handler higher. The handler ships in its own package:
                    </p>
                    <p class="client-install"><span>Install</span><code>{otelInstall}</code></p>
                    <p>
                        In <code>telemetry.py</code>, replace the last line, the one that adds a <code>LoggingHandler</code> to the root logger, with this block. The root logger then has one OpenTelemetry handler:
                    </p>
                    <div class="code-file">
                        <p class="code-file__name">telemetry.py</p>
                        <pre><code set:html={code(otelCode)} /></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        <code>log.debug("cart has 3 items")</code> now prints locally only, <code>log.info("order 1042 placed")</code> prints and is exported, and urllib3 lines below WARNING go nowhere. Records logged inside an active span carry its trace and span IDs. Recent SDK releases deprecate the <code>LoggingHandler</code> in <code>opentelemetry.sdk._logs</code> in favor of this package; both take the same <code>logger_provider</code> argument.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        With the zero-code <code>opentelemetry-instrument</code> setup, the SDK removes its handler while <code>basicConfig</code> or <code>dictConfig</code> runs and adds it back afterwards, so those calls still set the level.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="severity" class="scroll-mt-24">How levels map to OpenTelemetry severities</h3>
                    <p>
                        OpenTelemetry records each log's level twice: as a severity number from 1 to 24 and as severity text. The handler renames two of Python's levels:
                    </p>
                    <div class="guide-table">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Python level</th><th>Severity text</th><th>Severity number</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {[...levels].reverse().map((l) => (
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="lvl-badge" style={`--lvl: ${l.color}`}>{l.name}</span></td>
                                        <td><code>{l.otel}</code></td>
                                        <td class="mono">{l.severity}</td>
                                    </tr>
                                ))}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        So in Maple you filter on <code>WARN</code> and <code>FATAL</code>, not <code>WARNING</code> and <code>CRITICAL</code>. Levels between the standard ones get the matching step, so 25 exports as severity 12. A custom level below 10 exports with an unspecified severity number.
                    </p>

                    <h3 id="maple" class="scroll-mt-24">Filter by severity in Maple</h3>
                    <ol class="guide-steps">
                        <li>
                            <span>01</span>
                            <div><h4>Open Logs and pick a severity</h4><p>The volume chart stacks log counts by severity, in the colors this page uses for each level. In the sidebar, include or exclude values under <strong>Severity</strong>, and pick your app under <strong>Service</strong>.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure"><img src="/screenshots/guides/python-logging-levels-01-severity-filter.webp" data-zoomable alt="The Logs page for a Python service filtered to the WARN and ERROR severities: the volume chart stacked by severity and the matching log rows." width="2368" height="1180" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <span>02</span>
                            <div><h4>Open a record and its trace</h4><p>Click a row, then <strong>Open detail</strong>. ERROR and FATAL records show an <strong>Error</strong> or <strong>Fatal</strong> banner. For a record logged inside a span, the <code>trace:</code> link in the meta strip opens the request it belongs to.</p>
                                <figure class="guide-figure guide-figure--narrow"><img src="/screenshots/guides/python-logging-levels-02-log-detail.webp" data-zoomable alt="The detail sheet of an ERROR log from a Python service: the Error banner, the meta strip with the trace link and span ID, and the log attributes." width="1024" height="1400" loading="lazy" /></figure></div>
                        </li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="guide-maple">
                        <p class="guide-kicker">Python logs in Maple</p>
                        <h3>Send your logging records to Maple</h3>
                        <p>The Python guide sets up the handler, the exporter and trace correlation. Using Loguru instead of <code>logging</code>? See the <a href="/guides/loguru">Loguru guide</a>.</p>
                        <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
                            <a href="/docs/guides/instrumentation-python" class="guide-button guide-button--primary">Set up Python with Maple</a>
                            <a href="/docs/explore/logs" class="guide-button">Logs docs</a>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
                    <p class="guide-eyebrow"><span>05</span> · Mistakes</p>
                    <h2>Common Python logging level mistakes</h2>
                    <div class="guide-checklist">
                        {mistakes.map(([title, body], index) => (
